{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a soft pink snow blanketing the cobblestones. The air smelled of incense and matcha. I wandered through Fushimi Inari’s endless red torii gates, each one a whispered prayer, lost in the quiet hum of footsteps and distant temple bells.\n\nIn Tokyo, the city pulsed like a living circuit board. Shibuya’s scramble crossing was chaos choreographed to perfection. I ate ramen at a tiny stall in Shinjuku, slurping noodles so hot they made my eyes water—chef nodded, satisfied. At Tsukiji Outer Market, I tried uni on rice: briny, creamy, unforgettable.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was still. A single origami crane, folded by a child, rested beneath the Atomic Bomb Dome. I sat for an hour, silent.\n\nIn Nara, deer bowed for senbei. One nuzzled my palm like an old friend. I laughed, and it laughed back—well, as much as a deer can.\n\nEverywhere, kindness was quiet but deep. A woman in Osaka noticed me staring at a map, then walked me three blocks to my ryokan. “You’re welcome,” she said, bowing before vanishing into the crowd.\n\nI slept in a traditional inn, tatami floors cool under bare feet, futon soft as clouds. At dawn, I sipped green tea on the veranda as mist curled over the garden. No one spoke. No one needed to.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—and if you listen, it shows you beauty in stillness, precision in imperfection, grace in the ordinary.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of souvenirs—but my heart carried something heavier: peace.\n\nThree weeks. One lifetime.\n\n— *Japan, 2023*", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob"